  "note": "Since Nov 2026 the primary basis is the ACS organic content map (read in full); every articulation is answered (scripts/accm_maps.py), with units 9 (physical organic, practice) and 10 (organometallics) added for those nothing reached. Families follow the reaction chapters of Bruice's Organic Chemistry (a textbook, not a standard; chapter titles 2-17 from memory). The reactions and substrates in each are a house list. Every product is mass-balanced against its reactant by the generator; regiochemistry and stereochemistry were checked by eye."
 },
 "big_ideas": [
  {
   "id": "RULES",
   "title": "A few rules predict most products",
   "text": "Electrons flow from the electron-rich site to the electron-poor one, toward the more stable intermediate, and equilibria run to the weaker acid. Most of a two-semester course is those three sentences applied."
  },
  {
   "id": "REAGENT",
   "title": "A reagent has a job",
   "text": "Read a reagent as what it does (adds H and OH anti-Markovnikov, removes two hydrogens, swaps OH for a leaving group), not as a name to match to a product."
  },
  {
   "id": "BALANCE",
   "title": "Atoms are conserved",
   "text": "Know what each reagent adds to or removes from the molecule. A product that does not balance is wrong before you look at where anything went."
  }
 ],
 "practices": [
  {
   "id": "P1",
   "title": "Predict",
   "text": "Draw the major product on paper from the reactant and the reagents, in under a minute."
  },
  {
   "id": "P2",
   "title": "Read the reagent",
   "text": "Say what a reagent does to a functional group, in a few seconds."
  },
  {
   "id": "P3",
   "title": "Apply a rule",
   "text": "Use a counting or comparison rule to decide a direction, a site or a pathway."
  }
 ],
